What is APR?APR stands for Annual Percentage Rate. It's a measure used to compare the yearly cost of borrowing money or earning interest on an investment across different products or services in the financial market. The APR includes not only the interest rate but also any additional fees or costs associated with the transaction, providing a more comprehensive view of the total cost of borrowing.

For example, if you're taking out a loan, the APR will include the interest rate plus any origination fees, closing costs, and other charges that are part of the loan. The higher the APR, the more expensive it is to borrow money or earn interest on an investment. 

It's important for consumers to understand what APR means when they're considering taking out a loan or credit card, as it can significantly impact their overall cost of borrowing.yes
